window.docContents[3].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___10-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___10-Integrals--___4___10_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Exponentials":{"contents":"\\int e^{az}\\,dz=\\frac{e^{az}}a,\\qquad a\\ne0.For nonzero complex a, the displayed quotient is an antiderivative.\n\n\\int\\frac{dz}{e^{az}+b}=\\frac{az-\\ln(e^{az}+b)}{ab},\\qquad a,b\\ne0.Away from zero and the nonpositive-real branch cut of the logarithm argument,\nthe principal-logarithm expression is an antiderivative for nonzero complex\na and b.\n\n\\int\\frac{e^{az}-1}{e^{az}+1}\\,dz=\\frac2a\\ln(e^{az/2}+e^{-az/2}),\\qquad a\\ne0.On a branch avoiding zero and the nonpositive-real cut of the logarithm\nargument, the principal-logarithm expression differentiates to the displayed\nquotient.\n\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-cx^2}\\,dx=\\sqrt{\\frac\\pi c},\\qquad \\Re c>0.The Gaussian integral converges for complex c with positive real part; the\nprincipal complex square root gives the value.\n\n\\int_0^{\\ln2}\\frac{x e^x}{e^x-1}\\,dx=\\frac{\\pi^2}{12}.The finite integral up to ln 2 has value π²/12; its endpoint behavior is\nunderstood through the improper integral represented by the interval integral.\n\n\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{dx}{e^x+1}=\\ln2.The improper integral over the positive half-line equals ln 2.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.10 Integrals","header":"§4.10(ii) Exponentials","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___10-Integrals/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___10-Integrals--___4___10_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Exponentials"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___31-Special-Values-and-Limits/#chapter-4-section-31":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §4.31, Special Values and Limits.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.31. §4.31 Special Values and Limits","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___31-Special-Values-and-Limits/#chapter-4-section-31"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___11-Relations-to-Other-Functions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___11-Relations-to-Other-Functions--___6___11-Relations-to-Other-Functions--Confluent-Hypergeometric-Function":{"contents":"Tricomi's confluent hypergeometric function is denoted by U(a,b,z). In the\nspecial parameter case (a,b)=(1,1), the exponential integral is obtained by\nremoving the factor e^{-z} from the expression below. The identity uses the\ncanonical principal-E₁ map, the named tricomiU11 interface, and the explicit\nprincipal domain.\n\n\n\nE_{1}(z)=e^{-z}U(1,1,z)$$\n\nFor the auxiliary sine and cosine integrals, the DLMF uses functions f(z)\nand g(z) whose complex combination is naturally expressed with the same\nTricomi function. The argument is rotated by -iz; the imaginary unit, the\nparameters (1,1), and the sign are part of the identity. The canonical §6.2\nprincipal combinations appear through the named auxiliaryF, auxiliaryG, and\nnormalized tricomiU11 interfaces.\n\n\n\n\n\ng(z)+\\mathrm{i}f(z)=U(1,1,-\\mathrm{i}z)$$\n\nThe page notes that E3 is obtained from the auxiliary-function formula in\n§6.5.7. The explicit domain condition applies the principal continuation to\nthe rotated Tricomi argument.\n\nend LMLFManual.Chapter6\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.11 Relations to Other Functions\u0009§6.11 Relations to Other Functions","header":"Confluent Hypergeometric Function","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___11-Relations-to-Other-Functions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___11-Relations-to-Other-Functions--___6___11-Relations-to-Other-Functions--Confluent-Hypergeometric-Function"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--Sine-and-cosine-integrals":{"contents":"The second group uses entire removable-integrand definitions for Si and\nCin, while si and Ci retain improper tail/path meanings. The\nprincipal logarithm appears in the Ci/Cin relation, and real limits\nare stated on the positive ray.\n\n \\operatorname{Si}(z)=\\int_0^z\\frac{\\sin t}{t}\\,dt.\nSi(z) is the entire integral of sin t/t from zero to z.\n\n \\operatorname{si}(z)=-\\int_z^\\infty\\frac{\\sin t}{t}\\,dt=\\operatorname{Si}(z)-\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\nFor z ≠ 0, a regular path avoiding only the origin gives the sine tail;\nits negative is si(z), and DLMF records si(z)=Si(z)-π/2.\n\n \\operatorname{Ci}(z)=-\\int_z^\\infty\\frac{\\cos t}{t}\\,dt.\nCi(z) is the negative of the corresponding cosine tail on the principal path.\n\n \\operatorname{Cin}(z)=\\int_0^z\\frac{1-\\cos t}{t}\\,dt.\nCin(z) is the entire integral of (1-cos t)/t from zero to z.\n\n \\operatorname{Ci}(z)=-\\operatorname{Cin}(z)+\\ln z+\\gamma.\nOn the principal branch, Ci(z)=-Cin(z)+log z+γ.\n\n \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\operatorname{Si}(x)=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\qquad\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\operatorname{Ci}(x)=0.\nAlong the positive real axis, Si(x) tends to π/2 and Ci(x)\ntends to zero.\n\nThe source's notation note covers the Si, si, Ci, and Cin names; this page\ndistinguishes their entire and improper definitions explicitly.\n\nSi and Cin are entire, and Si is odd, whereas the tail definitions of si and\nCi require the path and convergence specifications shown above.\n\nThe Ci tail path follows the same principal-cut avoidance convention as E₁;\nits singular endpoint is excluded explicitly.\n\nThe Cin integrand has a removable value at zero and defines an even entire\nfunction, as reflected by the line-integral definition.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.2 Definitions and Interrelations\u0009§6.2 Definitions and Interrelations","header":"Sine and cosine integrals","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--Sine-and-cosine-integrals"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___5-Functional-Relations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___5-Functional-Relations--___5___5_LPAR_iii_RPAR_-Multiplication":{"contents":"Source: DLMF §5.5(iii).\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009§5.5 Functional Relations","header":"§5.5(iii) Multiplication","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___5-Functional-Relations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___5-Functional-Relations--___5___5_LPAR_iii_RPAR_-Multiplication"}});